
Part 1: Node.js - Quick Revision Notes
What is Node.js?
- JavaScript runtime built on Chrome's V8 engine
- Runs JS outside the browser
- Enables backend/server-side development with JavaScript
Why Use Node.js?
- Non-blocking, asynchronous I/O
- Event-driven architecture
- Fast & scalable
- Large ecosystem (npm)
HTTP Server with Node.js:
const http = require('http');
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
 res.setHeader("Content-Type", "text/html");
 res.end("<h1>Hello World</h1>");
});
server.listen(3000);
Built-in Node Modules:
- fs: File System - read/write files
- path: Handles file paths
- http: Create server
- os: Info about OS
- events: EventEmitter for async events
File System Example:
const fs = require('fs');
fs.writeFileSync('hello.txt', 'Hello from Node!');
Command Line Arguments:
node greet.js Ayush
const name = process.argv[2];
Node Project Setup:
npm init -y # Initialize package.json
node app.js # Run file
Routing (Basic):
if (req.url === "/about") {
 res.end("About Page");
}
